===Etymology===
From {{etyl|enm}} {{term|fecchen|lang=enm}}, from {{etyl|ang|en}} {{term|feccan|feċċan|to fetch|lang=ang}}, apparently an alteration of {{term|fetian|lang=ang}}, {{term|fatian||to fetch, marry|lang=ang}} (whence also English {{term|fet|lang=en}}), from {{proto|Germanic|fatōjanan||fatjanan|to fetch|lang=en}}, from {{proto|Indo-European|ped-|foot|lang=en}}. Cognate with {{etyl|nl|-}} {{term|vatten||to catch, grasp, understand|lang=nl}}, {{etyl|de|-}} {{term|fassen||to grasp, touch|lang=de}}, {{etyl|fo|-}} {{term|fata||to grasp, understand|lang=fo}}, {{etyl|is|-}} {{term|feta||to go, step|lang=is}}. More at {{l|en|foot}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{enPR|fĕch}}, {{IPA|/fɛtʃ/}}, {{SAMPA|/fEtS/}}
*: {{audio|en-us-fetch.ogg|Audio (US)}}
* {{rhymes|ɛtʃ}}

===Verb===
{{en-verb|fetches|fetching|'''[[fetched]]''', ''or {{archaic}}'' '''[[fetcht]]'''}}

# To [[retrieve]]; to bear towards; to go [[get]].
#* '''1908:''' [[w:Kenneth Grahame|Kenneth Grahame]], ''[[w:The Wind in the Willows|The Wind in the Willows]]''
#*: When they got home, the Rat made a bright fire in the parlour, and planted the Mole in an arm-chair in front of it, having '''fetched''' down a dressing-gown and slippers for him, and told him river stories till supper-time.
# To obtain as price or equivalent; to sell for.
#: ''If you put some new tyres on it, and clean it up a bit, the car should '''fetch''' about $ 5.000''
# {{nautical}} To bring or get within reach by going; to reach; to arrive at; to attain; to reach by sailing.
# {{intransitive}} To bring one's self; to make headway; to veer; as, to fetch about; to fetch to windward.

